    "## 12. Interview preparation: what to ask and how to explain the work\n",
    "\n",
    "**Two high-value Slack questions:**\n",
    "\n",
    "1. \"Is `discharge_summary_count` calculated strictly from information available *before* the prediction date? Its strong relationship with the label made me concerned about potential outcome leakage.\"\n",
    "2. \"Some patient IDs appear twice with slightly different clinical values. Are these separate snapshots, and is there a timestamp or preferred rule for choosing the prediction-time record?\"\n",
    "\n",
    "**Save for the follow-up call:**\n",
    "\n",
    "* **What is the prediction index date?** Which events and lab values are known by then? Can we test forward in time rather than with a random holdout?\n",
    "* **How many patients can a care team contact?** Choose top-100 or top-10% metrics around actual capacity rather than an arbitrary 0.5 threshold.\n",
    "* **What would success look like?** Captured future admissions, interventions accepted, avoidable admissions reduced, or resource allocation improved?\n",
    "* **How fresh are the records and which data are systematically missing?** Data availability may vary by healthcare partner.\n",
    "* **Do we need calibrated probabilities or just rankings?** Validate reliability across states, ages, sex and condition groups before deployment.\n",
    "\n",
    "**60-second walk-through:**\n",
    "\n",
    "\"I treated this as a patient-prioritization problem rather than simply predicting a binary label. I first audited duplicates, missingness and suspicious variables. Discharge-summary count looked like a possible shortcut to the target, so I excluded it until its timestamp is confirmed. I kept one record per patient, normalized inconsistent categories and fit imputers only inside training. I compared a linear baseline with a tree-based model without hyperparameter search, selected on a development set and reported the performance on unseen patients. Beyond ROC-AUC, I checked PR-AUC, score reliability and how many admissions appeared in the highest-risk 10%. Finally, I retrained on all labeled patients and generated one risk score for each of the 5,000 new patients. Before deployment I would focus on temporal validation, calibration, fairness and actual care-team capacity.\""
